# The AI that reads a medicine pack.
#
# Aushadh asks an OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint: a model running on this machine
# (Ollama, LM Studio) or a hosted one. Copy this file to `.env` and fill it in — `.env`
# is never committed, and the values are read at build time.
#
# With a model on this machine, nothing leaves the device. With a hosted one, the pack
# photo and the name you type go to that provider.

# A model on this machine, which needs no key:
EXPO_PUBLIC_AI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
EXPO_PUBLIC_AI_MODEL=llava

# Or Google AI Studio (Gemma), which speaks the same dialect and reads images. Make a key
# at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey and use its OpenAI-compatible endpoint:
#
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_AI_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_AI_MODEL=gemma-4-26b-a4b-it
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_AI_API_KEY=<the key>
#
# `gemma-4-26b-a4b-it` reads a pack in a few seconds; `gemma-4-31b-it` is stronger but
# slow enough to risk the app's timeout; `gemini-flash-lite-latest` is on the same URL and
# is the quickest of the three.

# These are only the defaults the app is built with. Everything prefixed EXPO_PUBLIC_ ships
# inside the app, so treat a built-in key as public. Leave it out, and each user can type
# their own endpoint, model and key in Settings, where they are kept on the device and
# replace these. Restart with `npx expo start --clear` after editing this file — the
# bundler caches the transform that bakes these values in.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_AI_API_KEY=
